 the books  Viserys also pinches her breast heart  and twists show left it out but then at  the end of the episode after their  wedding called Drogo and dresses her  again repeating the word know which is  the only word he knows in the common  tongue and this continues as Daenerys  trembles in fear and at no point  indicates that she wants this well in  the books the scene ends with Daenerys  consenting to sex with Drogo with Drogo  asking No and Dany knowing it was a  question that time and Dany teaching him  a new word yes now I'll admit I liked  the book version a lot better it's her  first step from being a victim to taking  more control of her story and the  showrunners did take some heat for  changing it but one could argue it does  fit in better within the theme of the  episode winter is coming  like if Danny began this episode as a  victim of sexual abuse by her brother  and then ended it with his victory of  her turning the tables on Drogo that  would kind of conflict with the idea of  all these characters plights becoming  worse instead we get to see Danny  gradually transition to more of her  conquer her self as this season goes on  I'm not defending the change of the  books I can just see why this episode  wanted to leave all their main  characters in these awful situations now  speaking of Khal Drogo one little detail  that I liked from him was this Dothraki  phrase he says while watching his men  fight at his wedding this is actually a  chant from the traditional Maori haka  war dance which Jason Momoa used during  his audition and he says it got him the  role and then later language consulted  David J Peterson who created the spoken  languages of Dothraki and Valyrian for  Game of Thrones retro actively worked in  eat a waka into the Dothraki language so  that it translates to testier might  basically he made it so that Khal Drogo  is the guy watching the fights in mortal  combat now the director of this episode  was Tim van  he directed the pilot and Episode two  but no other episodes on the show HBO  brought him in after he directed a ton  of episodes of The Sopranos the wire and  Boardwalk Empire then Patton is  considered a master of visuals and  pacing and one thing he does really well  is frame specific shots so that a  character is linked with the instrument  of their death in one of my favorite  shots in Boardwalk Empire Van Patten  pointed the camera downward as mr.  Harrow lowered his rifle seemingly  disarming himself but then he angles the  rifle back upward directly up into the  lens of the camera the point of view of  the other guy in the standoff the camera  angle lines up the death blow and then  Van Patten does a similar thing in the  pilot a game of Thrones early on when  the deserter is brought to the chopping  block he's framed within the concave  divot of that block with the blood of  past condemned men still staining the  wood the camera angle lines up the death  blow and then now I should say that Tim  Van Patten was not the original director  on the Game of Thrones pilot there was  an entirely different unaired version by  another director which according to  Benioff and Weiss was so bad that they  had to reshoot 90% of it it's actually  pretty interesting to examine what was  changed and why so let's run through  these adjustments okay starting with  this opening sequence notice these shots  of the Nights Watch brothers leaving  Castle black the original pilot simply  began with them finding the dead  wildlings in the haunted forest and in  that version the wildlings were just  kind of piled up not arranged in this  creepy pattern also the White Walkers  were originally more visible with the  script describing their frozen skin and  how they spoke to each other in a weird  language course the final version left  the White Walkers shrouded in darkness  we don't really get a good look at them  until the end of season 2 The effect of  this is that the White Walkers are left  as a more mysterious threat with their  carnage resembling something more like a  psychotic serial killer would do the  original pilot also had a different  opening credits sequence that version  showed an old man writing a message and  strapping it to a raven with the Ravens  soaring over the continent of Westeros  and landing on the Iron Throne actually  this shot of the raven landing at  Winterfell with the news of Jon Aaron's  death might be a nod to that title  sequence now one of the biggest changes  to the pilot was the addition of the  stark family dynamic Catelyn was recast  from Jennifer Ehle - Michelle Fairley  and the archery scene the hair brushing  scene the shot of Ned and Captain  bed together and the scene with the boys  getting their hair cut were all added to  contextualize the Starks as a family  unit bene huffin Weis later lamented  that the haircutting scene was in their  opinion the most cringeworthy scene in  the entire series all the actors in the  scene reportedly tried to out macho each  other by doing a bunch of sit-ups and  then they held their breath to flex  their abs during the scene wouldnt think  about the scene isn't really that  necessary they're just getting their  hair cut  they also reassigned the line our way is  the old way from Ned to brand this way  bran is characterized as a kid who has  already begun to understand his father's  wisdom by the time his father has to  immediately leave him next notice the  first words that we hear Jaime speak to  his sister in the pilot that's your  brother I feel it's my duty to warn you  you worry too much a huge problem with  the original pilot was that apparently  viewers didn't understand that Jaime and  Cersei were brother and sister and that  their affair was incestuous so this  whole conversation between Cersei and  Jaime was added in the second pilot  originally Cersei was talking to pycelle  here to clarify this relationship  further the showrunners also added this  random voiceover dialogue between Sansa  and Arya that's Jaime Lannister pleased  to impress you please shut up  notice we never see the actresses say  these lines that's because they dubbed  it over in post-production also with  that Jon Arryn funeral scene the actor  plain Jon Arryn is John standing a  well-known British actor he only makes a  cameo as a corpse in this version but  originally the old pilot showed his  death a scene that standing called  lunatic Jon Arryn was going to crawl out  of bed across the room and reach for ink  to write something but then he would  spill the ink all over himself and  Cersei would come in the room and stomp  on his hand the original pilot also  contained a flashback to the death of  Ned's brother Brandon stark and a cameo  by george RR martin as a pentose a  nobleman at Danny's wedding with a weird  hat actually think this is a character  that he played originally before it was  reshot and recast by the way they did  this mostly to recast Daenerys from  Tamzin merchant to Emilia Clarke okay  let's close out by talking about the  deeper meaning of this episode so the  episode is titled winter is coming the  stark family words but also a primary  thematic promise of the series and a  marketing slogan used over and over  throughout the show's run but what does  it really mean here when you think about  it the phrase winter is coming is  paradoxical on Game of Thrones  the opening sequence takes place in a  setting where winter has clearly arrived  it's not coming it's here and season  after season as winter descends over  Westeros still winter is coming are the  words that's because for the Starks as  cold and as dark as things currently  seem something colder and darker is  always coming there's always a way for  your circumstances to get even worse so  prepare no character this episode  improves their situation the stark  family gets split up as Ned heads into a  deadly political game he's not prepared  for John is left out in the cold brand  becomes crippled Searcy and Jamie's  secret affair becomes compromised Dany  is forced into a marriage with the  foreign stranger and none of these  characters began the episode with  particularly fortunate days either  things were already pretty bleak a white  Walker attack an execution a dead hand  of the king a dead dire wolf orphans and  exiles can embed yet still on top of all  this winter is coming and as we'll see  over the coming episodes and the coming  seasons Game of Thrones will find new  and shocking ways to deliver on that  promise where even when characters take  one step forward they take two steps  back worsening and worsening their  situations  no one ever succeeds they merely survive  and at a great cost because on Game of  Thrones winter just keeps coming  which of these fallen characters do you  miss the most now as much as I love Sean  Bean on this show Ned Stark was I think  more powerful to the series in his death  meanwhile Michelle Fairley's Catelyn  Stark and mark Addie's Robert Baratheon  were huge reasons I think that first  season worked and I don't know if the  show ever really recovered from their  losses and which character do you think  changed the most from the series  premiere to the season 7 finale now of  course Jamie Lant okay you know what  it's just brand right  you went from a boy to a tree weigh in  in the comments below and also let us  know what kind of Game of Thrones video  you want to see next like more of these  retro breakdowns like this do you want  me to continue with season 1 episode 2  or jump around to more game-changing  episodes like their lore or Blackwater  red wedding mountain versus the Viper  hardhome as I said we cover a lot of  stuff I can't guarantee that we'll cover  all this so it'll really depend on what  videos you guys want to watch and by the  way if you want to have it even bigger  say in what kind of videos we make here  you can join our team by contributing to  us on patreon big thanks to all of our  cur